theory.intro:
A **sector** is the region between two radii and the arc joining them. Those radii cut the full turn at the centre into the **interior** angle, inside the sector, and the **exterior** angle, in the rest of the circle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-60:3.4);
\coordinate (B) at (60:3.4);
\draw (O) circle (3.4);
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\draw[very thick] (A) arc[start angle=-60,end angle=60,radius=3.4];
\pic[draw,"interior",angle radius=0.9cm,angle eccentricity=2.1] {angle=A--O--B};
\pic[draw,"exterior",angle radius=2.6cm,angle eccentricity=0.62] {angle=B--O--A};
\node at (1.9,1.2) {sector};
\fill (O) circle (1.6pt);
\node[left] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
When a sector is more than half the circle its interior angle is **reflex**, larger than $180^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
